{"id":"references/MorganTian/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch01_Preliminaries#def:local-isometry","title":"def:local-isometry","path":"references/MorganTian/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch01_Preliminaries.md","line":868,"body":"","kind":"definition","status":"not_started","tex":"references/MorganTian/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch01_Preliminaries.tex#def:local-isometry","uses":["[[references/MorganTian/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch01_Preliminaries#def:riemannian-metric]]"],"inspired_by":[],"bindings":[],"is_index":false,"body_html":"","formal":[],"tex_content":{"path":"references/MorganTian/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch01_Preliminaries.tex","label":"def:local-isometry","line":2211,"source":"\\begin{definition}[Local isometry]\n\\label{def:local-isometry}\n\n\\uses{def:riemannian-metric}\n\n\nA smooth map $F\\colon (N,h)\\to (M,g)$ between Riemannian manifolds\nof the same dimension is a {\\sl local isometry} if $F^*g=h$, i.e.,\nif $dF_x\\colon T_xN\\to T_{F(x)}M$ is a linear isometry for every\n$x\\in N$. By the inverse function theorem, a local isometry\nrestricts to an isometry from a sufficiently small neighborhood of\neach point onto its image. An {\\sl isometry} is a bijective local\nisometry; its inverse is then also an isometry.\n\\end{definition}","html":" <p></p><section class=\"theorem\"><div class=\"theorem-label\">Definition</div></section>[Local isometry] <span class=\"tex-label\" id=\"tex-label-6465663a6c6f63616c2d69736f6d65747279\"></span> <p></p><a class=\"wikilink tex-use\" href=\"#node=references%2FMorganTian%2Fblueprint%2Fsrc%2Fchapters%2FCh01_Preliminaries%23def%3Ariemannian-metric\">↗ def:riemannian-metric</a> <p></p>A smooth map <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$F\\colon (N,h)\\to (M,g)$\">$F\\colon (N,h)\\to (M,g)$</span> between Riemannian manifolds of the same dimension is a local isometry if <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$F^*g=h$\">$F^*g=h$</span>, i.e., if <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$dF_x\\colon T_xN\\to T_{F(x)}M$\">$dF_x\\colon T_xN\\to T_{F(x)}M$</span> is a linear isometry for every <span class=\"math\" data-display=\"false\" data-tex=\"$x\\in N$\">$x\\in N$</span>. By the inverse function theorem, a local isometry restricts to an isometry from a sufficiently small neighborhood of each point onto its image. An isometry is a bijective local isometry; its inverse is then also an isometry. ","warnings":[]},"previous":"references/MorganTian/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch01_Preliminaries#lem:klingenberg-loop","next":"references/MorganTian/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch01_Preliminaries#lem:local-isometry-exponential","relations":[{"kind":"uses","id":"references/MorganTian/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch01_Preliminaries#def:riemannian-metric","title":"def:riemannian-metric","label":"def:riemannian-metric"}],"backlinks":[{"kind":"uses","id":"references/MorganTian/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch01_Preliminaries#modelpolar","title":"modelpolar","label":"modelpolar"},{"kind":"uses","id":"references/MorganTian/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch01_Preliminaries#lem:local-isometry-exponential","title":"lem:local-isometry-exponential","label":"lem:local-isometry-exponential"},{"kind":"uses","id":"references/MorganTian/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch01_Preliminaries#lem:local-isometry-normal-balls","title":"lem:local-isometry-normal-balls","label":"lem:local-isometry-normal-balls"},{"kind":"uses","id":"references/MorganTian/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch01_Preliminaries#lem:local-isometry-rigidity","title":"lem:local-isometry-rigidity","label":"lem:local-isometry-rigidity"},{"kind":"uses","id":"references/MorganTian/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch01_Preliminaries#lem:local-isometry-covering","title":"lem:local-isometry-covering","label":"lem:local-isometry-covering"},{"kind":"uses","id":"references/MorganTian/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch01_Preliminaries#lem:model-polar-isometry","title":"lem:model-polar-isometry","label":"lem:model-polar-isometry"},{"kind":"uses","id":"references/MorganTian/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch01_Preliminaries#lem:pullback-ball","title":"lem:pullback-ball","label":"lem:pullback-ball"},{"kind":"uses","id":"references/MorganTian/blueprint/src/chapters/formalization-support#def:isometry","title":"def:isometry","label":"def:isometry"},{"kind":"uses","id":"references/MorganTian/blueprint/src/chapters/formalization-support#lem:local-isometry-covering-connected-source","title":"lem:local-isometry-covering-connected-source","label":"lem:local-isometry-covering-connected-source"},{"kind":"uses","id":"references/MorganTian/blueprint/src/chapters/formalization-support#rem:local-isometry-local-inverse","title":"rem:local-isometry-local-inverse","label":"rem:local-isometry-local-inverse"}],"copy_context":{"version":1,"level":0,"node":"references/MorganTian/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch01_Preliminaries#def:local-isometry","title":"def:local-isometry","tex":"\\begin{definition}[Local isometry]\n\\label{def:local-isometry}\n\n\\uses{def:riemannian-metric}\n\n\nA smooth map $F\\colon (N,h)\\to (M,g)$ between Riemannian manifolds\nof the same dimension is a {\\sl local isometry} if $F^*g=h$, i.e.,\nif $dF_x\\colon T_xN\\to T_{F(x)}M$ is a linear isometry for every\n$x\\in N$. By the inverse function theorem, a local isometry\nrestricts to an isometry from a sufficiently small neighborhood of\neach point onto its image. An {\\sl isometry} is a bijective local\nisometry; its inverse is then also an isometry.\n\\end{definition}","source":"tex/references/MorganTian/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch01_Preliminaries.tex","description":"","dependencies":[{"node":"references/MorganTian/blueprint/src/chapters/Ch01_Preliminaries#def:riemannian-metric","title":"def:riemannian-metric","description":""}],"dependencies_omitted":0,"unresolved_dependencies":[],"unresolved_omitted":0}}